    <string name="extra_free_kbytes">extra_free_kbytes(KB)</string>
    <string name="extra_free_kbytes_desc">Specifies extra physical memory kept by the Linux kernel. When memory is low, compression to SWAP/ZRAM starts immediately; until memory recovers, the device slows down noticeably.\n\nSimply put, higher extra_free_kbytes = more aggressive memory reclaim (swap or kill), but don\'t set too high to avoid wasting physical memory and overloading disk/CPU!</string>
    <string name="watermark_scale_factor">watermark_scale_factor</string>
    <string name="watermark_scale_factor_desc">Range 0-1000; 1000 means 10%, indicating the difference between low/min/high watermarks as % of RAM.\n\nExample (my phone):\npageSize=4KB\nmanaged=1951743(pages)\nmin_free_kbytes=10900(KB)\nwatermark_scale_factor=500\n\nmin=10900/4=2725(pages)\nlow≈1951743*5%+2725≈100312(pages)\nhigh≈1951743*10%+2725≈197899(pages)\nNote: final low/high may be affected by extra_free_kbytes\n\nIn this example:\nmin=2725=10MB\nlow=100312≈392MB\nhigh=197899≈773MB\n\nMeaning: memory reclaim starts below 392MB until it exceeds 773MB~</string>
